package templates

// name_collision_warning.templ -- the 409 response body for a name edit that
// would collide with an existing artist's identity (#2730).
//
// Rendered instead of the generic ErrorToast because a bare "another artist
// already uses this name" leaves the operator with no next step.
//
// THE COPY IS DELIBERATELY NON-COMMITTAL ABOUT THE MERGE, and this is the
// subtle part. The obvious wording -- "merge them on the duplicates report" --
// is a promise the report cannot always keep. It fails in TWO unrelated ways
// that look identical to the operator:
//
//  1. Conflicting MBIDs. DetectDuplicates REFUSES to group two artists bound
//     to different non-empty MusicBrainz IDs (makeGuardedUnion in
//     internal/artist/duplicates.go, the #2527 data-loss guard). This guard has
//     no such condition, so such a pair is refused here and never forms a
//     group at all.
//  2. An ignored group (#2798). The duplicates page filters server-side
//     ignores (FilterIgnoredGroups), so the pair DOES exist as a group but is
//     hidden from the operator who ignored it.
//
// Either way the operator clicks through to a page that does not show the pair.
//
// The guard intentionally still refuses the conflicting-MBID rename. Nothing
// in this repo verifies that a stored MBID is CORRECT -- validation checks
// UUID shape only, and #2715 records that some stored MBIDs come from an
// unscored name-search hit -- so exempting on "different MBIDs" would defer to
// a signal that may itself be wrong. The report's refusal is a conservative
// stance about an irreversible merge, not an assertion that the two artists
// are distinct.
//
// So the copy promises only what is ALWAYS true: that a collision exists and
// WHICH artist it is with. It points at the report as the place to combine
// them, names the ignored-groups list as the next place to look, and otherwise
// says Stillwater is treating the two as separate -- true in all three cases,
// and stated without the operator needing to know what an MBID is or to recall
// having ignored anything.
//
// Styling reuses the ClobberWarnings warning-banner treatment verbatim (same
// amber palette, same rounded-md/px-4/py-3 box, same role="alert"). No new
// banner color, badge, or affordance is introduced.

// NameCollisionWarningView is the view model for the collision banner. The handler
// builds it from artist.NameCollision; the template never imports the artist
// domain package, matching the ArtistDuplicateMember convention above.
type NameCollisionWarningView struct {
	// ExistingName is the STORED name of the artist that already holds the
	// identity -- never the name the operator typed. The two differ whenever
	// the identity key folds a difference between them: "the cure" collides
	// with a stored "The Cure". Echoing the typed value back would name an
	// artist that does not exist under that spelling and would destroy the
	// one piece of information this message exists to carry, so
	// handlers_name_collision_guard_test.go pins it with a fixture whose
	// requested and stored names differ.
	ExistingName string
	// ExistingPlatformOnly is true when the colliding artist exists only as a
	// platform record with no directory on disk.
	ExistingPlatformOnly bool
	// DuplicatesURL is the base-path-qualified link to the duplicates report.
	DuplicatesURL string
}

templ NameCollisionWarning(w NameCollisionWarningView) {
	<div class="rounded-md bg-yellow-50 dark:bg-yellow-900/50 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-yellow-700 dark:text-yellow-300" role="alert">
		{ tf(ctx, "name_collision.already_used", w.ExistingName) }
		if w.ExistingPlatformOnly {
			{ t(ctx, "name_collision.existing_platform_only") }
		}
		{ t(ctx, "name_collision.resolve_hint") }
		{ " " }
		<a class="underline font-medium" href={ templ.SafeURL(w.DuplicatesURL) }>
			{ t(ctx, "name_collision.merge_link") }
		</a>
	</div>
}
